<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Somavamshi dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">882 CE – 1110 CE (approx.) · capital Yayatinagara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A dynasty of western and coastal Odisha that built the Lingaraja and Mukteshvara temples of Bhubaneswar.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Somavamshis were a branch of the lunar-line dynasty earlier known as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/panduvamshis-of-dakshina-kosala">Panduvamshis of Dakshina Kosala</a>. In the late 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janamejaya">Janamejaya</a> I established them in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, and his successors took over the coastal lands from the declining <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bhauma-kara-dynasty">Bhauma-Kara dynasty</a>, uniting much of Odisha.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their capital moved between Yayatinagara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajpur">Jajpur</a>. The kings were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivas</a> who granted land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, and tradition credits King Yayati Keshari with bringing Brahmins from the north. The temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhubaneswar">Bhubaneswar</a> reached maturity under their rule: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukteshvara-temple-bhubaneswar">Mukteshvara</a> temple and the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingaraja-temple">Lingaraja</a> temple, with its soaring tower, date from this period, as does the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmeswara-temple">Brahmeshvara temple</a> built by Queen Kolavati.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by invasions from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalachuris-of-tripuri">Kalachuris of Tripuri</a>, the Somavamshis were conquered around 1110 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anantavarman-chodaganga">Anantavarman Chodaganga</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-ganga-dynasty">Eastern Ganga dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 882 CE – 1110 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:77.25%;width:4.54%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>882 CE – 1110 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yayatinagara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Janamejaya I, Yayati I, Udyotakeshari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shaivism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Odia</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Somavamshis of Odisha, Kesari dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=996&amp;focus=somavamshi-dynasty" aria-label="Somavamshi dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6765 1316 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Somavamshi dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7313 1677h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7085 1572h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7085 1572h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=996&amp;focus=somavamshi-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
